"""Distinct FAQ intents and explicitly hypothetical planning examples for legacy routes."""
FAQS=[
('/faq-furniture.html','STR furniture replacement and spare-inventory FAQ',[
('When should rental furniture be replaced?','Use observed condition, suitability and the applicable product instructions. Record the actual problem and evaluate whether a supported repair restores the intended use. A calendar age alone does not establish that every item must be replaced. If a safety or structural concern is unresolved, remove the item from guest use as appropriate and obtain qualified assessment rather than treating a cosmetic refresh as a remedy.'),
('What should a replacement specification contain?','Keep the item’s function, dimensions, material or upholstery designation, care information and required relationship to the room. Include the delivery route and installation requirements. An approved substitute should preserve the guest task and usable capacity, not merely resemble the old product in a photo. Identify who can approve a change and retain the approval with the purchase record.'),
('Which items belong in spare inventory?','Start with items whose absence interrupts an essential routine and which can be stored and maintained appropriately. Identify compatibility and location, then review actual replacement history. A generic stockpile can tie up funds without solving the common interruptions. Keep clean textile spares separately identifiable from used items and document how stock moves between properties.'),
('How do cleaners report a furniture problem?','Use a clear item identifier, room, observed condition and photographs where useful. State whether the issue prevents the agreed guest use and identify the owner’s response route. Avoid asking the cleaner to make an unsupported diagnosis or improvise a replacement. Record the eventual action so repeated problems become visible in the operating history.'),
('How can one discontinued piece be replaced?','Compare the original functional specification with available alternatives. Check the complete arrangement, finish care and installation conditions. A new bedside table or chair can alter access and seated use even if its visual style is similar. Approve the specific variant and update the room record so future purchases do not rely on the discontinued identifier.'),
('What belongs in the financial handoff?','Retain the original item record, replacement invoice, location, removal or disposal information where relevant, and installation description. Keep these records available to the owner’s accounting and tax professionals. The design team should describe what was bought and done; it should not infer tax treatment from a shopping-list label.')]),
('/faq/furniture/','Choosing and specifying STR furniture FAQ',[
('Where should furniture selection begin?','Begin with the guest brief, retained inventory and measured room plan. Define the activities the furniture must support, such as group dining, reading or unpacking. Product shopping follows those requirements. A collection of attractive individual items does not automatically create a usable room or a complete guest-ready property.'),
('How do I compare seating capacity?','Test the complete occupied arrangement using actual product dimensions. Include nearby surfaces, door movement and routes. A nominal seat count or a staged photograph does not establish how the room works for your intended group. Check the actual seated positions and review the result when a product or layout changes.'),
('What does a useful product schedule include?','Record the exact item and variant, supplier, quantity, dimensions, finish, destination room and care information. Add delivery and installation responsibilities, approved alternatives and the date of approval. Keep retained items in the same room schedule so purchasing decisions can be checked against the full arrangement.'),
('How should material suitability be checked?','Read the exact product’s intended-use and care information, request a sample where appropriate and involve the operating team in a permitted cleaning trial. A broad label such as durable or performance does not answer every care, warranty or installation question. Retain the evidence that supports the selection rather than relying on the label alone.'),
('Who verifies dimensions before ordering?','Name the person responsible for site measurements and identify how the design team resolves discrepancies. Include the delivery path and relevant packaging information with the room dimensions. Confirming that the assembled furniture fits the floor plan does not establish that it can reach the room or be installed under the actual conditions.'),
('When is the furnishing assignment complete?','Use a written acceptance standard for the chosen scope. It may be a complete design handoff, assembled furniture or a stocked and tested rental. Record remaining work, responsibilities and dates on a punch list. A delivered box or a final rendering is not equivalent to every possible finished condition.')]),
('/faq-pricing.html','Comparing STR design quotes and fees FAQ',[
('How can two design fees be compared?','Give both candidates the same property information and required deliverables. Compare what the fee includes: design work, revisions, purchasing support, site visits, installation coordination and closeout. A lower fee may cover fewer tasks. Assign excluded work to someone and include its cost before comparing complete project totals.'),
('Which line items should be separate?','Request visible amounts or allowances for design, goods, tax, freight, receiving, storage, assembly, mounting, trades, household essentials and final styling as applicable. An omitted line remains an unanswered question. Keep estimates, allowances and fixed commitments distinguishable so an uncertain amount does not become an assumed cap.'),
('How should purchasing arrangements be described?','Ask who places orders, whose account is used, who pays vendors and who handles returns or damaged deliveries. Clarify any procurement fee or markup in the actual agreement. Keep product selections and approval records with the proposal so the owner can reconcile the goods received with the intended inventory.'),
('What should a change-order price show?','Describe the original scope, requested change, affected products or work, additional cost or credit, and effect on timing. Identify who must approve it before commitment. A conversation about a possible change should not be confused with authorization to buy or an accepted revised delivery date.'),
('Can a headline furnishing budget be used as a quote?','Only if the provider confirms that it covers your actual property and scope in writing. Published examples may involve different rooms, retained items, labor or delivery arrangements. Request the complete inventory and exclusions rather than extrapolating from an entry package or a selected project photograph.'),
('How do I document the final total?','Reconcile approved design and goods invoices, delivery and installation work, changes, credits and unresolved claims. Keep the final inventory and completion records with that reconciliation. Professional financial review can then use an evidence-based record of the actual project instead of a single headline budget.')]),
('/faq/pricing/','Building a complete STR furnishing budget FAQ',[
('What is the budget boundary?','State which rooms, retained items and finished condition the budget covers. Separate a focused refresh from a complete setup and identify work already funded elsewhere. A furnishing budget that excludes installation or household essentials should say so clearly. Define the boundary before deciding whether the total is adequate.'),
('How do I prioritize a limited budget?','List essential guest functions and observed condition problems first. Use a keep, repair and replace inventory to avoid replacing useful items automatically. Reserve funds for the work needed to make selected purchases usable, then consider optional styling. Defer an optional layer if it would leave an essential task unfinished.'),
('How should uncertainty be represented?','Keep committed costs, current estimates and unresolved amounts in separate columns. Identify the assumptions behind each estimate and the decision needed to replace it with a quote. Any uncommitted reserve should have a stated purpose; do not describe an arbitrary percentage as a universal rule for every rental.'),
('Does the budget need to include owner time?','At minimum, identify the work the owner will perform: measurements, order tracking, receiving, assembly coordination and acceptance. If comparing a self-managed plan with a managed proposal, make that workload visible. The owner can decide how to value it, but leaving it unnamed makes the two approaches difficult to compare.'),
('How do substitutions affect the budget?','Recheck item price, quantity, delivery, installation and any return or cancellation costs. Evaluate whether the substitute still meets the room’s functional and care requirements. A lower-priced item is not automatically a saving if it creates additional work or requires changes elsewhere in the room.'),
('What should be reviewed after completion?','Compare the original plan with the actual final inventory and costs. Record which assumptions were wrong, which changes were necessary and which purchases did not solve the intended problem. Use that record to improve the next property brief rather than treating one project’s total as a universal market benchmark.')]),
('/faq-process.html','STR design change-control and approval FAQ',[
('What counts as an approved design decision?','Keep a dated record of the specific plan, product or finish and who approved it. A mood board, an exploratory message and a purchase authorization serve different purposes. Identify the version that controls ordering and installation so teams do not act on different screenshots of the same room.'),
('When is a substitution acceptable?','Compare the proposed substitute with the original functional specification, dimensions, care requirements, cost and timing. Identify any related decisions it changes. Obtain the required approval before ordering, then update the product schedule. A visually similar item can still change usable seating, access or cleaning.'),
('How should an owner-requested change be handled?','Describe the requested result, affected work, additional cost or credit and timing implications. Check whether earlier purchases can be changed under their actual terms. Keep the decision in one written record so a casual discussion does not become an assumed instruction to spend or alter the schedule.'),
('What happens when measurements conflict?','Pause the affected decision, identify the source of each measurement and assign a person to verify the site. Update the approved plan and communicate the revision to anyone ordering or installing related items. Do not resolve a discrepancy by selecting whichever dimension makes the preferred furniture appear to fit.'),
('How are unresolved issues tracked?','Use a punch list with item, room, observed issue, responsible party, required action and agreed date. Attach photographs or product details where they clarify the problem. Separate essential guest-use issues from optional refinements so the acceptance decision is explicit rather than implied by a photo-ready appearance.'),
('Which records remain after handoff?','Retain approved plans, product variants, substitutions, care instructions, installation records, claims and final acceptance information. Give the operating team the version that matches the installed property. Those records make later replacements and financial reconciliation easier than a folder of unlabelled design images.')]),
('/faq/process/','STR design project sequence and handoff FAQ',[
('What should happen before design concepts?','Prepare a measured property brief, photographs, retained inventory, intended guest and operating constraints. Identify unresolved building conditions and the responsible parties. The designer needs reliable information about the assignment before a concept can become a useful layout or product schedule.'),
('When should furniture be ordered?','Order after the required measurements, product specifications, site conditions and approvals are resolved for the item. Confirm delivery and receiving arrangements. Some decisions may proceed while others remain open, but keep those dependencies explicit rather than treating the whole project as equally ready.'),
('How do construction and furnishing schedules connect?','List the work that must be complete before delivery or installation, including access, protection and storage arrangements. Identify who confirms readiness. A vendor dispatch estimate is not the same as a confirmed receiving date, and a receiving date does not establish that the property is ready for assembly or styling.'),
('What belongs in a remote design handoff?','Provide room plans, exact product variants, quantities, dimensions, care information, placement and unresolved interfaces. Name a local measurement verifier and execution lead. Include the substitution and question process so installers do not have to infer the plan from a visual board alone.'),
('What should be checked before photography?','Close the essential punch list and repeat the agreed guest-use and turnover checks. Confirm that advertised furniture and amenities are present and usable. Photograph the completed arrangement guests will receive, and distinguish any planning renderings from actual property photographs.'),
('How does the process continue after launch?','Observe operating reports, record condition and replacement issues, and review whether the design supports the intended routines. Keep updates tied to evidence. A refresh can then address a defined problem instead of restarting the entire design whenever a decorative trend or product availability changes.')])]
CASES=[
('scottsdale-desert-modern','Scottsdale desert-modern rental planning example','An owner is planning a hypothetical group rental with a retained dining table, a patio and a desire for a restrained desert-inspired palette. No completed client project, measured revenue or guest testimonial is established by this example.','The proposed lounge and dining areas look generous when empty, but the occupied-chair test reveals an interruption to the patio route. The owner revises the furniture arrangement before adding decorative accents.','Test the indoor-outdoor route with the intended group using both settings. Record exposure and storage observations for outdoor products, then evaluate the actual sample palette in the shared room’s daylight and evening light.','A coordinated entry-to-patio plan, a usable group-meal arrangement and a material/care record for the chosen furnishings.',['/blog/airbnb-outdoor-space-design.html','/resources/guides/guest-capacity-furnishing-matrix/']),
('destin-beach-house','Destin beach-house rental planning example','A hypothetical family-oriented home has serviceable bedrooms but no clear routine for bags, footwear and towels after an outing. The planning exercise concerns guest flow and care, not a documented local property result.','The owner initially plans a new lounge sofa. Observing the arrival sequence instead suggests that entry storage and towel handling should be resolved first, while the existing sofa remains under review.','Walk the return-from-outing routine with the intended belongings. Check storage capacity, the route to laundry and the location of clean replacements. Obtain appropriate assessment for unresolved moisture conditions.','A defined wet-gear routine, a retained-furniture plan and a reset checklist that the operating team can repeat.',['/learn/wet-gear-entry-and-storage-plan/','/blog/airbnb-bathroom-design-guide.html']),
('charleston-historic-charm','Charleston-inspired rental refresh planning example','This hypothetical owner wants to retain distinctive existing woodwork and furniture while improving lighting and shared-room usability. The example does not establish the history, condition or regulatory status of a real building.','The first concept adds new furniture around retained pieces without a complete measurement record. The owner pauses selection and develops a plan showing the actual scale, routes and tasks before committing to purchases.','Verify retained-item dimensions and the room layout. Discuss any work affecting building fabric with the responsible qualified parties and applicable approval process. Test lighting and useful surfaces without relying on a styled photograph.','A measured refresh plan that identifies retained features, the work to be assessed separately and a realistic furnishing scope.',['/resources/guides/keep-repair-replace-audit/','/blog/best-lighting-for-airbnb/']),
('sedona-red-rock-villa','Sedona-inspired retreat design planning example','A hypothetical retreat concept uses landscape-inspired color and quiet shared spaces. It is an educational brief, not a real villa case study or a claim about local rental demand.','The palette looks cohesive on a digital board, but the actual finish samples compete with retained flooring. The owner tests a smaller accent direction before ordering a whole-room accessory set.','Review samples beside existing surfaces in daylight and artificial light. Test the reading and conversation arrangement with actual seating, keeping useful surfaces and storage clear.','A tested palette hierarchy, a usable retreat lounge and a concise replacement specification for important materials.',['/blog/modern-airbnb-design/','/blog/tropical-color-palette-guide.html']),
('lake-tahoe-lakefront','Lake Tahoe retreat furnishing planning example','A hypothetical owner is planning a group stay with outdoor belongings, shared meals and different evening activities. No lakefront property, completed installation or measured occupancy is verified here.','The initial plan prioritizes matching bedroom decor while the shared rooms have unresolved storage and seating. The owner shifts the first design review to group capacity and the arrival routine.','Test dining, lounge and storage together at the intended group size. Observe how wet or bulky belongings are handled under the actual maintenance plan and check lighting for different evening activities.','A guest-capacity matrix, an entry/storage plan and a shared-room layout that supports the intended stay.',['/learn/large-group-activity-zoning/','/learn/wet-gear-entry-and-storage-plan/']),
('joshua-tree-desert-retreat','Joshua Tree-inspired rental design planning example','This hypothetical project explores a restrained desert-inspired interior around retained furniture. It is not evidence of a completed property, location-specific performance or a verified client outcome.','The concept contains many small styling objects on the coffee and bedside tables. A guest-use test shows that those surfaces need to remain available for drinks, books and personal belongings.','Use a limited visual hierarchy and test the reset with the cleaning team. Review actual materials and lighting in the property rather than assuming a reference image establishes the installed result.','A coherent visual direction, clear guest-use surfaces and an unambiguous reset photograph.',['/blog/modern-airbnb-design/','/blog/airbnb-turnover-design-faster-cleaning/']),
('gatlinburg-mountain-cabin','Mountain-cabin group stay planning example','A hypothetical cabin brief includes group meals, equipment storage and bedrooms with different layouts. The example does not describe a verified Gatlinburg client project or a particular financial result.','The owner counts beds but has not tested the dining and lounge arrangements. The design review expands to the complete group routine before decorative cabin details are selected.','Test usable seating and routes with the intended group, inspect storage locations and commission each bedroom after dark. Record differences between rooms rather than assuming one bedroom specification fits every layout.','A complete capacity plan, bedroom-specific checks and a storage routine that protects the shared spaces.',['/blog/airbnb-bedroom-design-guide.html','/learn/large-group-activity-zoning/']),
('savannah-southern-cottage','Savannah-inspired cottage refresh planning example','A hypothetical cottage owner wants a warm visual direction while keeping serviceable furniture and limiting project disruption. No completed local project or verified guest review is represented.','The first shopping list replaces several retained pieces without explaining which guest problem the replacements solve. The owner creates a keep, repair and replace inventory before revising the budget.','Measure retained furniture, inspect condition and test material samples beside the existing finishes. Prioritize the specific functional problems and obtain appropriate assessment for unresolved building issues.','A bounded refresh scope, a clear spending sequence and a record of which existing items remain.',['/blog/how-to-decorate-airbnb-on-a-budget/','/resources/guides/keep-repair-replace-audit/']),
('nashville-urban-loft','Urban-loft rental zoning planning example','This hypothetical loft must support sleeping, relaxing and occasional work within connected spaces. It is not a documented Nashville property transformation or occupancy result.','A desk and accent chair fit the empty-room plan but interrupt the route when both are in use. The owner tests the occupied arrangement before selecting a smaller or differently located workspace.','Run sleeping, work and lounge tasks using actual dimensions. Check glare, power access, belongings and the reset routine. Describe the workspace accurately in the eventual listing.','A measured multi-use layout and a task-tested workspace that does not compromise essential circulation.',['/learn/studio-rental-zoning-without-extra-furniture/','/blog/airbnb-home-office-design.html']),
('park-city-ski-chalet','Ski-stay rental storage planning example','A hypothetical group rental needs a defined routine for bulky outerwear and shared evening use. No real Park City chalet, installed amenity or measured revenue is verified by this example.','The concept provides many decorative hooks but no tested capacity or reset method for the intended belongings. The owner reviews storage and the route to shared spaces as one design decision.','Walk the arrival routine with representative belongings and check the actual storage and maintenance conditions. Keep owner supplies separate and obtain appropriate professional guidance for any specialist installation or unresolved moisture issue.','A tested entry/storage plan, a clear operating handoff and a shared-room layout for the intended group.',['/learn/wet-gear-entry-and-storage-plan/','/blog/airbnb-outdoor-space-design.html'])]
COMPARISONS=[
('/compare/mybnbdesign-vs-diy-design.html','DIY Airbnb design: decide what work to own','The useful comparison is the work you can reliably perform, not a claim that one route always earns more. List concept development, measurements, product specification, purchasing, delivery, installation coordination and acceptance separately.','A hypothetical owner enjoys selecting furniture but cannot be present for receiving and installation. They retain concept input while requesting local execution support. Another owner with a reliable local team may need only a measured design handoff. The same design fee cannot be compared fairly until those responsibilities are visible.','Create a task ledger with time availability, relevant capability, local support and the consequence of an unresolved task. Resolve essential measurement and installation responsibilities before committing to products.','/blog/diy-airbnb-makeover-before-after-strategies/'),
('/compare/mybnbdesign-vs-wayfair-furniture-package.html','Wayfair sourcing for STRs: coordinate the complete room','If sourcing through a retailer or marketplace such as Wayfair, evaluate exact products and the project’s execution separately. A collection of available items is not automatically a coordinated installed room. Confirm the current seller, variant, terms and delivery arrangement for the actual order.','A hypothetical owner finds a coordinated bedroom collection, but the dresser and bed have different delivery conditions and the preferred nightstand becomes unavailable. The owner keeps a measured plan and substitution requirements so the replacement can be checked against the room instead of selected by a similar photograph.','Record exact variants, quantities, condition evidence, receiving contacts and applicable claims deadlines. Ask who handles assembly, mounting and household stocking, and include any necessary local work in the complete cost.','/resources/guides/quote-scope-comparison/'),
('/compare/mybnbdesign-vs-local-interior-designer.html','Local interior designer for STRs: define the site role','Local availability can help with site information and coordination, but it does not establish that every task is included. Ask how the designer handles the intended guest routine, retained furniture, purchasing and operating handoff. Evaluate the actual proposal rather than assuming a residential or rental label determines the result.','A hypothetical host needs help checking existing finishes and furniture before a refresh. A local site visit can provide useful evidence, while the owner keeps some purchasing responsibility. The brief identifies what the visit must produce and who converts observations into approved selections.','Request a written site-role description, the measurement-verification process, product documentation and responsibility for contractor interfaces. Keep care, replacement and turnover needs visible alongside the visual direction.','/resources/guides/scope-before-shopping/'),
('/compare/mybnbdesign-vs-professional-stager.html','Staged props or permanent STR inventory: compare the handoff','A temporary presentation and a permanent guest-ready inventory have different ownership and operating requirements. Ask which goods remain, which are hired or borrowed, and what happens after photography. A photograph of a complete room does not establish that all of its contents are included in the rental setup.','A hypothetical owner photographs a furnished lounge using temporary styling pieces. Before launch they reconcile every visible seat, table and amenity against the owned inventory, replace essential temporary items and revise the photographs if the final room differs. The handoff includes removal dates and the intended reset arrangement.','Keep a photographed-item inventory with ownership, return dates, condition responsibilities and replacement arrangements. Confirm the room guests receive matches the listing, then test the permanent arrangement during a turnover rehearsal.','/blog/airbnb-photography-guide.html')]
